<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tevaram</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as தேவாரம், Tēvāram</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tevaram is the name given to the first seven volumes of the Tirumurai, the twelve-volume canon of Tamil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaivism">Shaivism</a>: 796 hymns of 8,284 stanzas by the three most prominent Shaiva poet-saints, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambandar">Sambandar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appar">Appar</a> and Sundarar. The &quot;Tevaram trio&quot; are counted among the 63 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayanars">Nayanars</a>, the revered poet-saints of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Tevaram</b> of Cuntaramurti the poet asks Shiva, “Do you know proper Tamil?”; the scholar David Shulman reads the question as one about knowing how to love, and notes that to “know Tamil” could also mean to be a civilised being.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Among the Nayanars, Karaikkal Ammaiyar, probably of the 6th century, was one of the earliest; Appar and Sambandar in the 7th century and Sundarar after them composed the hymns later collected as the <b>Tevaram</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Tevaram</b> is the name given to the first seven volumes of the Tirumurai, the twelve-volume canon of Tamil Shaivism: 796 hymns of 8,284 stanzas by the three most prominent Shaiva poet-saints, Sambandar, Appar and Sundarar.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tevaram">Tevaram</a></li></ul></section>
